\chapter{Architecture}

\section{The shape}

<How the concepts of the previous chapter are arranged: layers, processes,
boundaries, what talks to what. A figure (tikz) earns its place here if it
shows the real shape and not a box diagram that flatters it.>

\section{Dependencies between concepts}

<Which concept may depend on which, and --- stated explicitly --- which
dependencies are FORBIDDEN and why (``the domain must not depend on its API
surface''; ``accounts must not know about transactions''). If nothing is
forbidden, no architectural decision has been made. A reader, and the AI,
should be able to tell from this section alone whether a reference crosses a
boundary it shouldn't.>

\section{Technology}

<The stack, and why. Keep it short; the detail belongs in the decision
records below.>

\section{Decision records}

<One subsection per significant decision. State the choice, the alternatives
considered, and WHY the others were rejected. A decision without its
alternatives is not a decision, it is a preference. This is the ``why'' that
lives WITH the code, versioned --- not in a docs/adr/ folder that drifts.>

\subsection{Decision: <title>}

<\textbf{Chosen:} ... \quad \textbf{Considered:} ... \quad \textbf{Rejected
because:} ... \quad \textbf{Consequence we accept:} ...>
